  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>John Ray</strong> is a 3,665-foot summit in Texas.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>October through April is the comfortable window here \u2014 summer temperatures at this elevation are dangerously hot, so if you must hike in summer, start at first light and carry more water than you think you need.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Lost Mesa, Coyote Bluff and Wolf Mountain \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
